        {
          "id": 2528829657,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "> block vs record\n\nI introduced the notion of \"records\" and record types as an abstraction of items that appear in arbitrary types of capture files. There's no one-to-one mapping between them and pcapng blocks - all three types of pcapng packet blocks (Enhanced, Simple, and the now-deprecated plain Packet Blocks) map to REC_TYPE_PACKET, and there could be record types that don't correspond to pcapng locks.\n\nREC_TYPE_FT_SPECIFIC_[EVENT|REPORT] are intended for records that correspond to something in a particular file type and only in that type.  If there's a type of item that appears in more than one file type, that would be something that should be a new record type - I'm not sure what non-pcapng files would use REC_TYPE_SYSCALL, but REC_TYPE_SYSTEMD_JOURNAL_EXPORT is used in both pcapng files and, well, journal export files from systemd. Even if there isn't an *exact* equivalence between items in different capture file types, if a common item to which all of the different items can be mapped without loss of information, and that can be written to the various file types without loss of critical information (some information loss may be inevitable when reading one file type and writing in another file type).",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
